WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.910
“I used the same password

2
00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:04.590
everywhere, but it was a strong one!”

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.470
A student told me that after her

4
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.670
account was taken over.

5
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.490
A small gaming forum she’d joined

6
00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.130
years ago had been hacked.

7
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.110
Her strong password was in the leak,

8
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.170
and someone tried it everywhere.

9
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.110
Today: how attackers get passwords,

10
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:23.990
and the defences that stop them.

11
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.010
Attackers get passwords in four main ways.

12
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.810
They guess the common ones: one

13
00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.230
two three four five six, names,

14
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.790
birthdays, football teams.

15
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.500
They try long lists of words.

16
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.470
They try every possible combination.

17
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.040
And they reuse passwords from leaks.

18
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.550
Guessing on a website is slow.

19
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.790
After a few wrong tries, a good site

20
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.090
makes you wait or locks the account.

21
00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.530
The real danger is a leak: when a site’s

22
00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.970
password file is stolen, attackers can

23
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.950
guess on their own computers, with no

24
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.210
limit.

25
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.230
Here’s where length matters.

26
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.680
Eight random lower-case letters give about

27
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.680
two hundred billion possibilities.

28
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.700
A gaming computer guessing ten

29
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.800
billion a second tries them all

30
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.100
in about twenty seconds.

31
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.210
Five random words would take the same

32
00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.310
computer about ninety years.

33
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.920
Think of a bicycle lock

34
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:25.920
with number wheels.

35
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.580
Each extra wheel multiplies

36
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.080
the combinations by ten.

37
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.820
A random word is like a wheel

38
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.540
with thousands of positions.

39
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:36.550
Quick check!

40
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.720
A site’s password file leaks.

41
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.790
Which lasts longest: a name and a

42
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.490
birth year, eight random letters,

43
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.950
or five random words?

44
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.820
Five random words.

45
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.410
Names and birth years are tried

46
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.610
first, so they fall almost at once.

47
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.330
Eight random letters sound strong, but a

48
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.290
fast computer gets through them in

49
00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:02.590
seconds.

50
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.950
Now one of the most common attacks: reuse.

51
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.560
Attackers take the emails and passwords

52
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.420
from one leak and try them on hundreds

53
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.900
of other sites, automatically.

54
00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.430
It’s called credential stuffing.

55
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.770
If you used the same password, every

56
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.370
account falls together, however strong it

57
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:23.590
was.

58
00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.630
Good websites defend you too.

59
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.870
They slow down or lock after

60
00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:30.930
too many wrong tries.

61
00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.730
They store a fingerprint of your

62
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.870
password, not the password itself.

63
00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.190
And they check new passwords

64
00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.070
against lists of leaked ones.

65
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.760
Your part is simple.

66
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.040
Use a long passphrase of random words.

67
00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:49.490
Use a different one for every account, and

68
00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.390
let a password manager remember them.

69
00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.640
Turn on two-step sign-in, so a leaked

70
00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.420
password alone isn’t enough.

71
00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.440
Your turn.

72
00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.340
A gaming site you used years ago

73
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.540
was hacked, and you used the same

74
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.560
password for your email.

75
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.100
Why does that matter?

76
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.710
Because attackers try every leaked

77
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.010
password on other sites, your email

78
00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:12.530
included.

79
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.160
So change it now, everywhere you used it.

80
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.360
Remember: long random words, one password

81
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.900
per account, and two-step sign-in.

82
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.460
Try it tomorrow: list your accounts on

83
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.600
paper, with no passwords on it.

84
00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.390
Mark every place you used the same

85
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.370
password, and change those first,

86
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:34.750
starting with your email.
